───────────────────────────────────────────────────
Cannot be established from this repository alone: depends on state only known at runtime (e.g. feature flags, request data).
The mechanism is clear, but observable impact requires runtime selection of the supplied-browser or built-in-browser path, actual configuration values, and local Chrome/port state. The diff does not show that any real caller uses a non-default port.

Browser configuration now supports selecting the Chrome remote-debugging port and consistently uses it when probing, launching, and connecting to Chrome. User-supplied Chrome binaries are now launched with an argument-vector subprocess API rather than a shell-oriented API. The agent and file-upload edits appear non-semantic. Actual deployment impact depends on callers selecting the affected browser setup paths and, for the new capability, configuring a non-default port.

Likely behavioral changes
  • Callers using a supplied Chrome binary can choose a remote-debugging port; browser reuse checks, the newly launched Chrome argument, and the CDP connection target use that configured port rather than a fixed endpoint.
  • A supplied Chrome executable is now started directly with separate executable and argument values. This avoids shell command interpretation and allows the generated remote-debugging option to be passed as an individual argument.
  • The built-in browser launch path now derives its remote-debugging argument and port-conflict check from BrowserConfig. If the configured local port is already occupied, that debugging argument is omitted as before, but for the selected port rather than specifically port 9222.

